Package: krauken
Architecture: all
Pre-Depends: debconf (>= 0.5) | debconf-2.0
Depends: ${misc:Depends},
 python3 (>= 3.11),
 python3-venv,
 build-essential,
 cmake (>= 3.10),
 git,
 python3-lgpio | python3-rpi-lgpio
Description: Fermentation chamber controller
 Control daemon, hardware supervisor, web API, and web UI for the Krauken
 fermentation controller -- drives BrewPi (Arduino) and Krauken hardware
 chambers, reads Tilt hydrometers, and serves a local web UI for
 monitoring and controlling a fermentation.
 .
 Architecture "all", not a real architecture: this package ships PYTHON
 SOURCE, not a pre-built venv or compiled extensions -- postinst creates
 the actual virtualenv and runs `pip install` natively on the machine
 being installed on, at install time, not at package-build time. This is
 a deliberate choice (see debian/postinst's own comment), not an
 oversight: Raspberry Pi OS's own pip is already configured to pull
 prebuilt wheels for compiled dependencies (pydantic-core, etc.) from
 piwheels.org, which correctly covers the target's real armv6l/armhf/
 arm64 architecture -- something a GitHub Actions x86_64 runner cross-
 building or QEMU-emulating a wheel itself cannot reliably guarantee,
 particularly for the original Pi/Pi Zero's armv6l, which most modern
 ARM cross-build tooling doesn't target at all.
 .
 python3-lgpio (or python3-rpi-lgpio, whichever Raspberry Pi OS's current
 repos actually provide -- NOT independently confirmed at packaging time,
 verify before relying on this) supplies GPIO access for BrewPi/Krauken
 hardware and krauken-hwid's EEPROM write-protect toggle. build-essential
 + cmake are for compiling the vendored raspberrypi/utils eeptools
 sources (also done in postinst, same "build natively on the target"
 reasoning) -- see krauken/cli/hwid.py's own module docstring.
